        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 844,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 676,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Computes the standard normal cumulative distribution function for every\nelement of a tensor: the probability that a normal value with mean 0 and\nstandard deviation 1 is below x.\n\nIt turns a z-score (a distance from the mean in standard deviations) into\na percentile.\n\nTHE CURVE:\nAn S from 0 to 1, passing 0.5 at 0: ndtr(1) = 0.841, ndtr(1.96) = 0.975,\nndtr(3) = 0.99865. It is erf rescaled: (1 + erf(x / 1.414)) / 2.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.special.ndtr\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nA tensor - or a number or a list, which is turned into one. Receiving it\ntriggers the node. Each element is worked on separately, so the output is\nthe same shape as the input. Whole-number input comes out as floats.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nThe result, element by element, in the same shape. On the same device\nas the input; float32 in gives float32 out.\n\nWHERE IT IS DEFINED:\nDefined for every real number. Below about -14 it underflows to 0 in\n32-bit floats; t.special.logndtr keeps going.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe metro re-clicks t.linspace ten times a second. t.linspace makes 200\nevenly spaced values from its start to its stop (-4 to 4 here), and the\nupper plot draws them: a straight ramp. The lower plot is ndtr of each\nof those 200 values, so it is the graph of the function from -4 to 4,\ndrawn from -0.05 (bottom) to 1.05 (top). Drag start and stop on t.linspace\nto look at another stretch.\n\nRELATED:\nt.special.ndtri undoes it. t.special.logndtr is its log, for the far\ntail. t.special.erf is the same curve on a different scale.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 660,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Computes the standard normal cumulative distribution function for every\nelement of a tensor: the probability that a normal value with mean 0 and\nstandard deviation 1 is below x.\n\nIt turns a z-score (a distance from the mean in standard deviations) into\na percentile.\n\nTHE CURVE:\nAn S from 0 to 1, passing 0.5 at 0: ndtr(1) = 0.841, ndtr(1.96) = 0.975,\nndtr(3) = 0.99865. It is erf rescaled: (1 + erf(x / 1.414)) / 2.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.special.ndtr\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nA tensor - or a number or a list, which is turned into one. Receiving it\ntriggers the node. Each element is worked on separately, so the output is\nthe same shape as the input. Whole-number input comes out as floats.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nThe result, element by element, in the same shape. On the same device\nas the input; float32 in gives float32 out.\n\nWHERE IT IS DEFINED:\nDefined for every real number. Below about -14 it underflows to 0 in\n32-bit floats; t.special.logndtr keeps going.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe metro re-clicks t.linspace ten times a second. t.linspace makes 200\nevenly spaced values from its start to its stop (-4 to 4 here), and the\nupper plot draws them: a straight ramp. The lower plot is ndtr of each\nof those 200 values, so it is the graph of the function from -4 to 4,\ndrawn from -0.05 (bottom) to 1.05 (top). Drag start and stop on t.linspace\nto look at another stretch.\n\nRELATED:\nt.special.ndtri undoes it. t.special.logndtr is its log, for the far\ntail. t.special.erf is the same curve on a different scale."
        },
